We investigate the impact of $b 	o u \ell  ar{
u}$ decay, particularly $B 	o \pi \ell 
u$, on constraints for new physics that affect the $b 	o c \ell  ar{
u}$ decay, especially $B 	o D \ell 
u$. In a model-independent approach, we establish relationships between the new physics couplings in $b 	o u \ell  ar{
u}$ and $b 	o c \ell  ar{
u}$ decays, taking into account the effects of anomalous couplings. We find that the current experimental data on $B 	o \pi \ell 
u$ and $B 	o D \ell 
u$ decays impose a direct relation between the vector couplings in these two transitions. This relation is consistent with the predictions of the Standard Model.
